%description:

RFC 826 -- an ARP reply that no request provoked is discarded, and provokes nothing.

Checks RFC826-RECV-10 from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc826/catalog.md, and covers
RFC826-RECV-1. The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/input-validation.md#an-unsolicited-reply; the
program steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the shared link mockup:
host A = hostA, host B = hostB, host C = hostC, switch S = switch.

Nothing in the run sends a datagram. A crafted reply reaches host B at 0.5 s, as if from the
link: its sender fields hold the addresses of host A and its target fields hold the addresses
of host B. Host B never asked for it. The opcode question of the reception algorithm has one
positive branch, the request, so a reply takes the negative branch: end of processing.

A reply that answered a real request would prove nothing, because a host that stayed silent
could be silent out of satisfaction. An unsolicited reply removes that ambiguity, and it is
also the shape an attacker uses.

No program can produce this packet. The address resolution module builds every ARP packet
itself, from its own addresses and from the address that routing gave it, so the packet is
pushed onto the interface of host B instead.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

static Packet *buildUnsolicitedReply(const CaptureStore&)
{
    return buildArpPacket("craftedReply", "hostB.eth[0]", ARP_REPLY,
            interfaceMacAddress("hostA.eth[0]"), interfaceIpv4Address("hostA.eth[0]"),
            interfaceMacAddress("hostB.eth[0]"), interfaceIpv4Address("hostB.eth[0]"));
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc826_unsolicited_reply")
        // Expected observation 1: the crafted reply arrives at host B, with the opcode of a
        // reply.
        .inject(inject("hostB").into("eth[0]", "upperLayerOut").at(0.5)
                  .describe("an unsolicited ARP reply, from host A to host B")
                  .packet(buildUnsolicitedReply))
        // Expected observation 2: host B puts no ARP packet on the link afterwards
        // (RFC826-RECV-10, RECV-1).
        .never(on("hostB")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return arpPacketLeaves(c.event, "hostB"); })
                  .describe("any ARP packet from host B")
                  .within(0.4));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc826_unsolicited_reply compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.ethernet.EthernetSwitch;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The shared link mockup: three hosts on one Ethernet segment, joined by a switch.
network ArpSharedNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        switch: EthernetSwitch { @display("p=400,150"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=250,250"); }
        hostC: StandardHost { @display("p=550,150"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostB.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostC.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpSharedNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# No host sends a datagram: the crafted reply is the only stimulus of the run.

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc826_unsolicited_reply: PASS

%contains: stdout
injecting craftedReply into ArpSharedNet.hostB.eth[0].upperLayerOut
